      "synth": {
        "answer": "Hume and Kant offer fundamentally opposed accounts of causation, a disagreement that drove Kant from his \"dogmatic slumber\" into the development of transcendental idealism. Hume argued that causal necessity is not an objective feature of the world but a subjective habit derived from experience; Kant rejected this reduction to custom, arguing instead that causality is an *a priori* category of the understanding necessary for any coherent experience at all.\n\n### Hume’s Account: Causation as Custom\nFor David Hume, the concept of cause is entirely empirical. He analyzed causation through five related issues: how we acquire the idea, what it means, how we reason about causes, whether they exist in nature or mind, and how we infer their existence [Source: hume-newton]. Hume concluded that our idea of \"cause\" does not refer to any observable power or \"glue\" connecting events (rejecting final and simultaneous causes) but consists only of three elements: priority in time, spatial contiguity, and constant conjunction [Source: causation-physics; Source: hume-newton].\n\nBecause no necessary connection can be observed between distinct events, Hume argued that the belief in causality arises from **custom** or habit. After repeatedly observing one type of event followed by another, the mind forms a subjective expectation that the second will follow the first [Source: kant-hume-causality; Source: hegel-dialectics]. Thus, causal necessity is psychological rather than ontological. While this view is often classified as skeptical because it removes objective \"making-things-happen\" force from nature, Hume provides what Saul Kripke calls a \"skeptical solution\": he does not deny causation exists but reduces it to patterns of association and mental inclination [Source: causation-law].\n\n### Kant’s Account: Causation as an *A Priori* Category\nKant famously stated that Hume’s skepticism regarding causality woke him from his \"dogmatic slumber\" [Source: sep]. However, Kant argued that Hume was correct only insofar as causal principles cannot be derived from experience; where Hume erred was in concluding that they therefore lack objective validity. Kant posited that the concept of cause is one of the **categories of the understanding**—specifically, a pure *a priori* concept that structures all possible experience [Source: induction-problem; Source: kant-hume-causality].\n\nFor Kant, causality involves **necessary connection** and **strict universality**, which empirical observation can never provide because experience only yields contingent regularities [Source: sep]. Instead, the mind actively imposes the rule of causality on sensory data to convert subjective temporal sequences into objective events [Source: sep]. This \"Copernican revolution\" suggests that knowledge does not revolve around objects as they are in themselves, but rather that objects of experience must conform to our cognitive structure [Source: hegel-dialectics]. Consequently, every event necessarily has a cause not because we observe this law in nature, but because without it, we could not have a unified experience of time or objectivity.\n\n### Why Kant Saw Hume’s Problem as Demanding a Transcendental Solution\nKant took Hume to have identified a genuine problem—that empirical induction cannot justify necessary connections—but rejected Hume’s solution (custom) as insufficient for science and metaphysics. Kant argued that the \"faculty of association\" consisting solely of sensory items cannot account for propositions requiring universality and necessity [Source: kant-transcendental]. If causality were merely subjective habit, scientific knowledge would lack objective validity.\n\nTherefore, Kant proposed a **transcendental argument** concerning the necessary preconditions of experience. He reversed the empiricist programme: rather than asking how concepts arise from experience, he asked what conditions must be true *for* experience to occur at all [Source: induction-problem]. The category of cause is such a precondition; it is required to synthesize the manifold of intuition into a coherent self-conscious subject (apperception) [Source: kant-transcendental]. By grounding causality in the structure of human reason rather than in external objects or internal habits, Kant aimed to secure the possibility of synthetic *a priori* knowledge, thereby saving science from Humean skepticism while limiting metaphysical claims to the realm of possible experience.\n\nI don't have reliable information on whether contemporary philosophers consider Kant's specific transcendental deduction successful in refuting modern variations of Humean skepticism; would you like me to search for current debates on this topic?\n\n---\n*Verification note: these statements could not be confirmed against your sources — treat them as unverified:*\n- Hume concluded that our idea of \"cause\" does not refer to any observable power or \"glue\" connecting events (rejecting final and simultaneous causes) but consist…\n- faculty of association",
        "reasoning_chars": 0,
        "stream_wall_ms": 65264,
        "total_latency_ms": 59732,
