      "vector_eligible": true,
      "synth": {
        "answer": "Aristotle’s hylomorphic account of substance posits that physical objects are compounds (*synola*) of matter and form. Matter is the potentiality to be a specific thing, while form is the actuality or essence that makes it what it is [Source: aristotle-metaphysics]. In *Metaphysics* Zeta, Aristotle argues that primary substance must be both \"separable\" (capable of independent existence) and a \"this something\" (*tode ti*, a determinate individual). While matter can exist independently in some sense (e.g., wood surviving a desk), it lacks definite individuality without form; conversely, form provides the unity and definition but cannot exist as an individual substance apart from its matter in natural cases [Source: aristotle-metaphysics]. Thus, form is identified as the primary principle of substantiality because it accounts for why a thing is what it is [Source: sep].\n\nIn *De Anima* II.1, Aristotle applies this framework to living beings by defining the soul as the \"first actuality of a natural body which has life in potentiality\" (*De Anima* ii 1, 412b5–6) or simply as the \"substance as form of a natural body which has life in potentiality\" (412a20–1) [Source: aristotle-psychology]. The relation between soul and body is thus an instance of the general matter-form relationship: the ensouled body is \"in-formed matter,\" where the soul acts as the organizing principle that accounts for all vital functions—nutrition, perception, and thought—in virtue of being the system of active abilities inherent to that organism [Source: ancient-soul; Source: dualism].\n\nCommentators are divided on whether this constitutes non-reductive hylomorphism or a kind of property dualism. \n\n**The Non-Reductive Form-of-the-Body Reading:**\nThis view holds that Aristotle steers a middle course between reductive materialism (which identifies the soul with matter) and Platonic substance dualism (which treats the soul as a separate entity). Proponents argue that because form is not identical to matter but rather determines it, the soul is distinct from the body yet inseparable from it in existence—much like shape cannot exist without bronze [Source: aristotle-psychology; Source: dualism]. As noted by Radulphus Brito following Aquinas, if the substantial form were removed, the subject would be named only equivocally (e.g., a dead horse is called \"horse\" only in name), proving the soul is an intrinsic perfection of the living body rather than an extrinsic addition or mere property [Source: radulphus-brito]. This reading emphasizes that Aristotle’s hylomorphism provides a unified explanatory framework where vital functions are performed by natural organisms due to their specific structural complexity and active abilities [Source: ancient-soul].\n\n**The Property Dualism / Immaterial Intellect Reading:**\nOther commentators point to passages suggesting dualist elements. While Aristotle generally claims the soul is not separable from the body, he appends the observation that certain parts of it may be separable because they \"are not the actualities of any part of the body\" (*De Anima* ii 1, 413a6–7) [Source: aristotle-psychology; Source: byzantine-philosophy]. This refers specifically to *nous* (intellect/mind). In Book III of *De Anima*, Aristotle describes an intellect that lacks a bodily organ and is \"alone immortal and eternal,\" arguing that if the intellect were in a physical organ, its sensitivity would be restricted just as the eye cannot hear sound [Source: dualism]. Because the intellect can think about all material forms without restriction, it must be immaterial. Some interpreters read this as a form of property or substance dualism where at least one faculty—the active intellect—is distinct from the body’s matter, potentially surviving death [Source: byzantine-philosophy; Source: medieval-philosophy]. This creates tension with his general hylomorphism, leading some to view him as holding two conflicting views: an embodied soul for most functions and an unembodied intellect for reasoning [Source: aristotle-psychology].\n\nI don't have reliable information on whether modern neuroscience has resolved the specific \"property vs. non-reductive\" debate regarding Aristotle's *nous*.",
        "reasoning_chars": 0,
        "stream_wall_ms": 72147,
        "total_latency_ms": 66027,
